<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frankfurt Rhine-Main</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rhine-Main</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> Rhine-Main is a metropolitan region of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> around the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and Main, including Frankfurt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiesbaden">Wiesbaden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a> and Offenbach. With about six million inhabitants it is one of Germany's main economic areas, home to banking, transport and chemical industries. Much of it lay within the former Grand Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frankfurt-rhine-main.jpg" alt="Frankfurt Rhine-Main" width="320" height="189" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Wolf, www.foto-tw.de · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frankfurter_Altstadt_mit_Skyline_2019_(100MP).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
